1. hello.bpf.c — Why do we need this?
hello.bpf.c
What is it?

This is the source code that you write for the eBPF program.

For example:

SEC("tracepoint/syscalls/sys_enter_execve")
int hello(void *ctx)
{
    bpf_printk("Hello eBPF!\n");
    return 0;
}
Why is it used?

Because you need to tell the kernel:

"What do I want to do when this particular kernel event happens?"

For example:

When execve() happens
        ↓
run my code
        ↓
print "Hello eBPF!"

So hello.bpf.c is essentially your instructions for what the eBPF program should do.

Why can't we skip it?

Because something has to define the behavior of your eBPF program.

You could write a different program, for example:

Monitor TCP connections
Monitor packets
Monitor processes
Monitor file access
Monitor CPU usage
Monitor system calls

The .bpf.c file contains the logic for that.

Simple analogy

Think of hello.bpf.c as a recipe.

hello.bpf.c
     ↓
Recipe:
"When event X happens,
do Y."
2. clang — Why do we need it?
hello.bpf.c
     │
     │ clang
     ▼
hello.bpf.o
What is Clang?

Clang is a C compiler.

You write code that humans can understand:

bpf_printk("Hello eBPF!");

But the computer/kernel cannot directly execute your C source code.

So Clang translates it into a form suitable for eBPF.

Human-readable C
       ↓
     Clang
       ↓
eBPF instructions
Why is it used?

Because the kernel doesn't want:

C source code

It needs:

compiled eBPF instructions

That's the job of Clang.

Very important

For an eBPF program you typically use:

clang -target bpf

The important part is:

-target bpf

It tells Clang:

"Compile this C program for the eBPF architecture."

3. hello.bpf.o — Why do we need this?
hello.bpf.o

This is one of the most important files.

What is it?

It is the compiled eBPF object file.

It contains things such as:

eBPF instructions
BTF/type information
section information
relocation information
other metadata

So:

hello.bpf.c
      ↓
    clang
      ↓
hello.bpf.o
Why is it used?

Because the kernel cannot load your original C source directly.

Instead, we give the loader the compiled eBPF object.

Why not directly load hello.bpf.c?

Because:

hello.bpf.c

is source code.

The kernel needs the compiled representation.

So:

Source code
     ↓
Compiler
     ↓
Compiled object
     ↓
Loader
     ↓
Kernel
Is hello.bpf.o eBPF bytecode?

At a beginner level:

Yes, you can think of it as containing the compiled eBPF bytecode/instructions.

More precisely, it is an ELF object file containing eBPF instructions plus metadata.

4. bpftool — Why do we need it here?

Next:

hello.bpf.o
     │
     │ bpftool
     ▼
hello.skel.h
What is bpftool?

bpftool is a command-line utility for working with BPF/eBPF.

It can do many things:

inspect BPF programs
inspect maps
inspect links
inspect BTF
generate skeletons
show loaded programs
etc.

In this particular step, we're using it to generate a skeleton.

For example:

bpftool gen skeleton hello.bpf.o > hello.skel.h
5. hello.skel.h — Why do we need it?

This is probably the most confusing part for beginners.

hello.skel.h

is a generated C header file.

It is created from:

hello.bpf.o

using:

bpftool
Why?

Because your user-space C program needs an easy way to interact with the eBPF program.

Without the skeleton, you'd have to write more low-level libbpf code yourself.

The skeleton gives your user-space program convenient functions for things like:

Open the eBPF object
Load it
Attach it
Access maps
Detach it
Destroy it

Conceptually:

hello.bpf.o
     ↓
 bpftool
     ↓
hello.skel.h
     ↓
hello.c
Simple analogy

Imagine hello.bpf.o is a machine.

The skeleton is like a remote-control interface for that machine.

Instead of manually controlling every internal component, your hello.c program can use convenient functions.

6. hello.c — Why do we need another C file?

This is extremely important.

You already have:

hello.bpf.c

Why do you need:

hello.c

?

Because they have different jobs.

hello.bpf.c

This is the eBPF program.

It eventually runs in:

KERNEL SPACE

Its job is:

"What should I do when the kernel event happens?"

hello.c

This is a normal user-space C program.

Its job is:

"Take my eBPF program and load/manage it in the kernel."

So:

hello.bpf.c
      ↓
WHAT THE EBPF PROGRAM DOES

while:

hello.c
      ↓
MANAGES THE EBPF PROGRAM

This distinction is fundamental.

7. Why does hello.c need the skeleton?

Because we typically write:

#include "hello.skel.h"

Now hello.c can use the functions generated by the skeleton.

For example:

skel = hello_bpf__open_and_load();

and:

hello_bpf__attach(skel);

So:

hello.c
   ↓
hello.skel.h
   ↓
libbpf
   ↓
hello.bpf.o
   ↓
Linux kernel
8. gcc — Why do we need it?

Now:

hello.c
   │
   │ gcc
   ▼
hello
What is GCC?

GCC is another C compiler.

It compiles your user-space C program.

This is different from compiling hello.bpf.c.

Remember:

hello.bpf.c
     ↓
clang -target bpf
     ↓
hello.bpf.o

But:

hello.c
     ↓
gcc
     ↓
hello

Why?

Because hello.c is a normal user-space C program, not an eBPF program.

9. hello — What is this?

After GCC:

hello

is the executable program you can run:

sudo ./hello

This program runs in:

USER SPACE

Its job is to control/load the eBPF program.

Think:

hello
  ↓
"I am the manager."

while:

hello.bpf.o
  ↓
"I am the eBPF program."
10. libbpf — Why do we need it?

Now we reach a very important component:

hello
   │
   │ libbpf
   ▼
Linux Kernel
What is libbpf?

libbpf is a C library for working with eBPF.

It makes it much easier for your user-space C program to:

open eBPF objects
load programs
attach programs
manage maps
manage links
handle BTF
etc.
Why is it needed?

Your normal user-space program cannot simply say:

"Kernel, please run this eBPF program."

It needs to communicate with the kernel using Linux's BPF mechanisms/system calls.

libbpf handles much of that complexity for you.

So:

hello.c
   ↓
libbpf
   ↓
Linux kernel
Simple analogy

Think of:

hello.c = you
Linux kernel = another country
libbpf = translator

Your C program says:

"Load this eBPF program."

libbpf translates that request into the appropriate kernel operations.

11. Linux Kernel — Why does the eBPF program go here?

This is the whole reason we're using eBPF.

The eBPF program needs to observe things that happen inside the operating system.

For example:

system calls
processes
network packets
TCP connections
file access
CPU activity
network interfaces

These things happen in or through the Linux kernel.

So eBPF programs are designed to run in kernel space.

USER SPACE
    │
    │ libbpf
    ▼
KERNEL SPACE
    │
    ▼
eBPF
12. eBPF Verifier — Why is this needed?

This is one of the most important safety mechanisms.

When your program arrives at the kernel, the kernel doesn't simply say:

"Sure, run whatever code you gave me."

Instead:

eBPF program
     ↓
Verifier

The verifier asks:

"Is this program safe to run inside the kernel?"

Why is this necessary?

Because kernel code is powerful.

A badly written program running unrestricted in the kernel could potentially:

crash the system
corrupt memory
access invalid memory
cause security problems

So Linux checks the eBPF program before allowing it to run.

Simple analogy

Imagine airport security:

You
 ↓
Airport security
 ↓
Safe?
 ↓
YES → allowed inside
NO  → rejected

Similarly:

eBPF program
      ↓
eBPF verifier
      ↓
safe?
  ↙       ↘
YES       NO
 ↓         ↓
load      reject
13. eBPF Program Loaded — What does "loaded" mean?

If the verifier accepts the program:

Verifier
    ↓
PASS
    ↓
eBPF program loaded

This means the kernel has accepted the eBPF program and prepared it for execution.

But loaded doesn't necessarily mean "executing right now."

This is an important distinction.

Think:

Loaded = program is installed/available in the kernel

but:

Executing = an event caused the program to run
14. Attach to Tracepoint — Why do we need this?

Your eBPF program needs to know:

"When should I run?"

That's what attachment provides.

Your code might contain:

SEC("tracepoint/syscalls/sys_enter_execve")

This means:

Attach me to:
sys_enter_execve

So:

eBPF program
     │
     │ attach
     ▼
sys_enter_execve
15. What is the purpose of a tracepoint?

A tracepoint is basically a predefined observation point inside the kernel.

For example:

Linux kernel
────────────────────────

Process
   ↓
execve()
   ↓
[ sys_enter_execve ] ← tracepoint
   ↓
continue execution

You put your eBPF program at that observation point.

So you can say:

"Whenever the kernel reaches this point, run my eBPF program."

16. Why don't we just run the eBPF program continuously?

Because that would be wasteful.

Imagine you want to monitor TCP connections.

You don't want your program constantly doing:

check TCP
check TCP
check TCP
check TCP
check TCP
...

Instead, you attach to an event:

TCP event happens
       ↓
eBPF executes

This is much more efficient.

17. Wait for Event — What does this mean?

After attaching:

eBPF program
     ↓
attached
     ↓
WAIT

The eBPF program isn't constantly running.

It waits for its associated event.

For our example:

sys_enter_execve

So it waits for a process to execute a program.

18. User runs ls — Why is this important?

Suppose you type:

ls

You might think:

I typed ls
     ↓
ls runs

But internally, Linux does much more.

The shell asks Linux to execute the program.

One of the system calls involved is:

execve()

So:

You type ls
     ↓
Shell
     ↓
execve()
     ↓
Linux kernel
19. execve() — What is it?

execve() is a Linux system call used to execute a program.

Conceptually:

"Please replace the current process
with this program."

For example:

bash
  ↓
execve("/usr/bin/ls")
  ↓
ls starts
20. sys_enter_execve — What happens here?

When the process enters the execve system call:

execve()
   ↓
sys_enter_execve

our tracepoint is triggered.

And because we attached our eBPF program there:

sys_enter_execve
       ↓
YOUR eBPF PROGRAM
21. eBPF program executes

Now the eBPF program finally runs.

For example:

int hello(void *ctx)
{
    bpf_printk("Hello eBPF!\n");

    return 0;
}

The kernel invokes it:

Event occurs
     ↓
Tracepoint
     ↓
eBPF program
     ↓
hello()
22. bpf_printk() — Why is it used?

Your eBPF program needs some way to produce output for debugging.

That's where:

bpf_printk()

comes in.

eBPF program
      ↓
bpf_printk()
      ↓
kernel tracing system

It is particularly useful while learning and debugging.

However, for your eventual TCP thesis, you generally won't use bpf_printk() for high-volume monitoring data. You'll learn to use mechanisms such as:

BPF maps
Ring buffers
Perf buffers

because they're much more appropriate for sending structured monitoring data to user space.

23. Kernel trace buffer — Why is it used?

The message from:

bpf_printk("Hello eBPF!");

needs somewhere to go.

It goes through the kernel's tracing infrastructure.

Conceptually:

eBPF
  ↓
bpf_printk()
  ↓
kernel tracing buffer
  ↓
trace_pipe
  ↓
terminal

You can observe it with:

sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/tracing/trace_pipe
24. Why do we need the trace buffer?

Because the eBPF program is running in kernel space, while you are looking at the result from user space.

The trace buffer provides a way to observe the debugging output.

Think:

KERNEL SPACE
     │
     │ bpf_printk()
     ▼
trace buffer
     │
     ▼
USER SPACE
     │
     ▼
terminal
